      '1933 (\'33 Act) The \'33 Act requires that nonexempt securities register with the federal government before being offered for sale to the public. The first step in the process is to file a registration statement. The registration statement contains key information about the security being issued, such as:\n\n• Details about the company\'s business operation\n• Information about the management of the company\n• description of the security being offered\n\nPlans for how the money being raised will be used\n\nFinancial statements certified by independent accountants Usually, this form is filed by the company issuing the securities with the help of an investment bank (a broker-dealer that specializes in underwriting securities). In most cases, the underwriter will purchase the securities from the issuer at a discount and then sell them to the public at a fixed public offering price. The Securities Act of 1933 has two main purposes: (1) to require companies to publicly disclose all relevant financial information about their securities prior to offering them for sale, and (2) to prohibit fraud and deceit in the sale of securities. The issuer is required to certify that the information in the registration statement is true and does not contain any misrepresentations of facts. Filing a false or fraudulent registration statement is a crime.\n\nPart of the registration statement also serves as a disclosure document to investors. This document is called a prospectus. The prospectus provides information that potential investors need to help them decide whether they want to invest in the securities being offered. Registration statements and other forms must be filed with the Securities and Exchange Commission (the SEC, which was established in 1934). Investors and the general public can access filings using the SEC\'s EDGAR (Electronic Data Gathering, Analysis, and Retrieval) database. Regulators examine the registration statement to make sure the legal requirements are met. Until the SEC determines that the security has met the registration requirements, the securities may not be sold. The SEC does not judge the merits of the securities. aa Test-Taking Tip: The Securities and Exchange Commission does not "approve" securities. It simply registers the securities and allows them to be sold. Once the SEC has registered the securities (the effective date), the securities may be sold to the public at the public offering price. This price must appear on the prospectus, and a copy of the prospectus must be sent to a customer at or prior to an offer or sale of any registered primary offering.',
